c\sb320\sl320\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 Shareware Program\par \'a9 1994 - 1996 John W Dawson\par \par \pard \s18\qc\sb320\sl320\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 V3.1F, September 1996\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 \sect \sectd \sbkodd\linemod0\cols1 {\header \pard\plain \s1\brdrb\brdrs \tqr\tx9720 \f16 {\b\f21 Address Book User Guide\tab Version 3.1F\par }}{\footer \pard\plain \s1\qc\brdrt\brdrs \f16 {\chpgn }\par }\pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 1\tab Introduction\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Address Book is an Application for the Psion Series 3A which you can use to manage addresses, 'phone numbers, and related information. It has a number of special features which make it more convenient and suitable for this purpose than the standard {\b Database} Application built into the Series 3A. These include:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab Alphabetic listing and searching of names;\par \bullet \tab Enhanced DTMF dialling:\par \pard\plain \s10\qj\fi-360\li1080\sa240\sl240\tx1080 \f16 \bullet \tab Variable volume;\par \bullet \tab Automatic prefixing of charge card account number and PIN;\par \bullet \tab Automatic prefixing of network service codes (for call diversion etc.);\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab Linking of records, to allow common information to be defined once only but shared by more than entry;\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab Configurable print layouts, allowing you to print addresses onto envelopes or labels. The program generates RTF files containing all the necessary formatting, which can be printed using a compatible word processor on a PC;\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab Tagging of entries, to create temporary or permanent lists of related entries;\par \bullet \tab Importing and exporting of all or selected parts of records.\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The Application manages one or more databases of information about individuals or organisations. Each entry in a database has the same layout, but the template for this is fully configurable.\par Two of the fields have special significance, since they are used to sort the entries. In the default template they have the labels {\b Name} and {\b Organisation}; however you can change their labels and positions in the template.\par An entry may relate to a person, in which case the {\b Name }field will be present and optionally an {\b Organisation. }Alternatively the entry may relate to a Company or other organisation, in which case there will no {\b Name}, only an {\b Organisation}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 There are four principle screens:\par The {\b\i Find} screen lists entries in alphabetical order, and allows alphabetic searches.\par The {\b\i Show} screen displays a single entry in full.\par The {\b\i Update} screen allows existing entries to be modified.\par The {\b\i Add} screen allows new entries to be added.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 2\tab Getting Started\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 2.1\tab Installation\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 To install the Application carry out the following steps:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 1\tab Copy the file {\b ADDBKV7.OPA} into the {\b \\APP} directory on the Internal Drive or an SSD.\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 2\tab On the System screen use the "Install" ({\b Psion-I)} command to install {\b ADDBKV}7. The Address Book icon (depicting a book, 'phone, and envelope) should now appear on the screen. \par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 3\tab You may find it convenient to assign a button to the Application, using the "Assign Button" {\b (Psion-A)} command.\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If you are upgrading from a previous version of Address Book then you should delete all .OPA and .OPO files installed for the previous version.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 2.2\tab Creating a new Address Book\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can now create an Address Book database by using the "New File" {\b (Psion-N)} command (while the highlight is beneath the Address Book icon). In the dialog box specify the name for the database and the drive where it is to be located.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\i Edit Labels} screen will now be displayed, allowing you to customise the default template to your preferences. If the default is acceptable just type {\b Escape. }Otherwise edit the labels; keys work in the same way as on the {\i Add} and {\i Modify} screens described below. Type {\b Enter} when finished.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 A dialog will then be displayed, to allow you to specify which fields to use for sorting, and the format you wish to enter names in. The choice of format is either forename(s) followed by surname (e.g. "Sally Ann Field") or surname followed by forename(s) (e.g. "Field,Sally Ann"). You can choose the character used to separate the forenames and surnames; this can be a space, a comma, or some other custom character. Alternatively you can specify "None", in which case names are not split into separate forename and surname parts. This last option would be most suitable if you wished to use {\b Address Book} to manage information other than names and addresses.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 On completion of the dialog the {\i Add }screen (described fully below) will then be displayed, enabling you to start adding entries to the database.\par Once you have some entries in the database you can go to the {\i Find }screen by typing {\b Escape} or {\b Diamond}.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 2.3\tab Using an existing Database\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If you have an address database in standard Psion DBF format (e.g. created using the built in Data application or an OPL program) then you can open this with {\b Address Book}. You can {\ul not} use a Database that contains numeric fields, but any Database which the built-in {\b Database} can open should be okay.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Note that the default directory and extension for {\b Address Book} is different from the built-in {\b Database} (for historical reasons), hence {\b Database} files will not appear under the {\b Address Book} icon. However you can create an 'alias' to access such files more conveniently. Position the highlight under the {\b Address Book} icon, and type {\b Psion-E}. Create a new list for {\b Address Book}, with {\b Directory} \\DAT and {\b Extension} DBF. Your database files will then appear under the additional {\b Address Book} icon.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Databases which have been created and maintained by {\b Address Book} have an extra 'index' file associated with them. When {\b Address Book} first opens a database where this index information is not available then, it must build this index. Once created there should be no need to rebuild this index, unless the index is accidentally deleted or the database file is modified by an application other than {\b Address Book}.\par Open the file in the normal way (from the System screen). If the database does not contain a template (typically a file created by an OPL application) then the {\i Edit Labels} screen is displayed. The default template is shown, and you should modify this to reflect the structure of entries in the database. (If there is a template already present in the database then {\b Address Book} will use this.)\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 A dialog is then shown to allow you to specify how the database is to be sorted and the layout of the {\b Name} field (as when creating a new file). There are extra dialog lines which allow you to control whether entries which contain more than one name in the {\b Name} field are split into multiple linked entries.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If you set {\b Name Separator} to "None" then no changes will be made to entries in your database. Otherwise if an entry contains more than one name (separated by the specified character) then extra database entries are created for the second and subsequent names, which are all linked to the entry for the first name. (See below for explanation of linking). E.g. if your database has an entry like:\par \pard\plain \s25\fi-1728\li1728\sl240\tqr\tx1440\tx1728 \f16 \tab {\b Name:}\tab John Brown\line Mary Bell\par \pard \s25\fi-1728\li1728\sl240\tqr\tx1440\tx1728 \tab {\b Address:}\tab 3, High St\line Any Town\line \par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 then if you set {\b Name Separator} to "New Line" two separate entries will be created for John Brown and Mary Bell.\par The application will now build the index. When this is complete the {\i Find} screen is displayed, listing the entries in alphabetic order.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 3\tab General Points\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Most of the basic features operate in the 'standard' way. {\b Diamond/Shift-Diamond} switches to the next/previous screen. {\b Menu} displays the menus. {\b Control-Menu }controls the displaying of the Status Window.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\i Find, Show, Update}, and {\i Add }screens can all be zoomed, by typing {\b Psion-Z} or {\b Psion-Shift-Z}. The "About Address Book" {\b (Psion-Shift-A) }command displays the introductory screen. You can exit cleanly from the Application by using the "Exit" {\b (Psion-X)} command in any screen.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 4\tab The Find Screen\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Entries are listed in alphabetical order. Entries for {\b People }are sorted by surname, which is generally taken to be the final space-separated 'word' in a name. E.g. John William {\b H}edges and Peter {\b H}etherington-Whyte would both be listed under {\b H}. \par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can override this, to cater for exceptions such as double-barrelled surnames without hyphens, e.g. Ralph Vaughan Williams which should be listed under {\b V}. You should enter such names with a double space between the last forename and the start of the surname (e.g. between Ralph and Vaughan).\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The \'c8 character indicates the 'current' entry.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Entries for {\b Organisations }are sorted by their complete names; e.g. {\b A}cme Software Company would be listed under {\b A}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can find an entry by typing the initial letters of its surname (or complete name for an organisation). Use lower case letters only. As you type the program will progressively find the first entry which matches the letters entered, and display the entry with the matched letters highlighted.\par Upper and lower case characters are treated as equivalent. Accented characters are sorted and matched as the equivalent unaccented character; "\'be" is treated as "a", "\'bf" as "o", and "\'a7" as "s". Punctuation characters are ignored when sorting and matching; e.g. O'Regan is listed between Oppenheimer and Oswald, and can be found by typing {\b o r ...}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If you make a mistake when typing the sequence of initial letters then press the {\b Escape} key; this cancels the matching, and enables you to start the sequence again from the first letter.\par Once the entry you require is displayed on the screen you can select it immediately by typing the digit or {\ul shifted} letter shown to the left of it. The entry is then shown in full on the {\i Show }screen. {\b Enter} or {\b Diamond} displays the current entry in full.\par You can move up and down the screen using the {\b Up/Down} arrow keys. {\b Page Up/Down} move a whole screenfull at a time. {\b Home} moves to the first entry, {\b End} to the last. Use of any of these keys cancels any matching in progress.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 4.1\tab Configuring the Find Screen\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can configure the layout of the {\i Find} screen by using the "Screen Layout" {\b (Psion-Shift-S)} command. {\b Surname width} and {\b Forename width }specify the widths of the two different parts of names (approximately in number of characters). {\b Extra field} specifies an additional field to be shown on the screen. If the 'phone symbol is selected then the first non-blank 'phone number of each entry is shown.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can also use this dialog to specify how tags are shown on the {\i Find} screen. A "*" can be displayed against each {\i Find} screen entry which has a particular tag set, or any tag set.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 5\tab The Show Screen\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This displays the full details for a single entry. Only fields which have information in them are shown. Any tags set can be shown, either as full  names or short codes (use {\b Psion-Shift-S} to set required preference).\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If the entry is too big to be shown in full then you can scroll up and down, using {\b Up/Down/Page-Up/Page-Down}. Alternatively you can zoom to a smaller font.\par Typing any lower case letter starts a 'quick-find'; the {\i Find} screen is displayed with the first entry starting with the letter selected.\par You can display the next or previous entry in the database (or found set) by typing ">" or "<" respectively.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 6\tab The Update Screen\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can update the current entry by using the "Update" {\b (Psion-U)} command on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screens, or by pressing {\b Diamond }on the {\i Show} screen. The {\i Update} screen is then displayed, showing all the fields for the entry and their current values if any.\par You can now change these values by typing text on the keyboard. If all or part of a field is highlighted then this is replaced by what you type; otherwise text is added at the cursor position. You can put line breaks in any of the fields by typing {\b Shift-Enter}.\par Use the arrow keys to move around within a field or between fields. Use these keys with {\b Shift} down to highlight text. {\b Control-Left/Right} moves a word at a time. {\b Home} and {\b End} take you to the start and end of the current line.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\b Delete} key removes the highlighted text, or the character to the left of the cursor if nothing is highlighted. {\b Shift-Delete} deletes to the right, and {\b Psion-Shift-Delete} deletes to the end of line. You can cut and paste text between fields using {\b Delete} or "Copy" {\b (Psion-C)} with text highlighted and then "Insert" {\b (Psion-I)}. You can "Bring" text highlighted in other applications by typing {\b Psion-B}. You can also use the "Bring" command in other applications to copy highlighted text from {\b Address Book}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Text is automatically wrapped onto the next line if a line becomes to long to fit on the screen.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\b Enter} key writes the modified entry back into the database. An entry will not be updated if both the {\b Name} and {\b Organisation} fields are blank. Use {\b Escape} to abandon the update, leaving the entry unchanged in the database.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 7\tab The Add Screen\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can add new entries to your database by using the "Add" {\b (Psion-A)} command or by pressing the {\b Diamond} key on the {\i Update} screen or {\b Shift-Diamond} on the {\i Find} screen. This takes you to the {\i Add} screen, whose operation is similar to the {\i Update }screen described above, except that all fields are initially blank.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can create a new entry based on an existing entry by using the "Copy Entry" {\b (Psion-O)} command.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 8\tab Finding Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The principle way of finding a particular entry is by alphabetical searching by name on the {\i Find} screen. However you can also make searches on other fields, using the "Find" {\b (Psion-F)} command. In the dialog you then specify the text you are looking for, which field to search (or all fields), whether the search should be case sensitive, and whether the entire field must match the specified text.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can either list all the entries which match the criteria (by setting {\b Find} to "All"), or display the first matching entry in full (by setting {\b Find} to "First").\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If "All" was selected then when the search is complete a dialog showing the number of matching entries is displayed. Type {\b Escape} to abort the search, or {\b Enter} to list the found set on the {\i Find} screen. The found entries can then be selected, displayed, modified, deleted, printed, etc., exactly as when the entire database is listed. Typing {\b Escape} lists the entire database again (a second {\b Escape} is required if part of a name is highlighted.) Also if any entries are added the program reverts to listing the full database.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If "First" was selected you can find further entries matching the same criteria using "Find Next" {\b (Psion-G)}. Note that "Find" and "Find Next" search the database in {\i physical} order, which may not be the same as the {\i sorted} order in which entries appear on the {\i Find} screen.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can find and list all entries linked to the currently selected entry by using the "Find Linked" {\b (Psion-K)} command. This works in similar fashion to searching with the {\b Find} = "All" option.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 9\tab Tagging Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can define up to 16 different "tags", and apply one or more of them to individual records, to mark the records as belonging to a subset of the database, or as being of special interest. You can then search for entries which have a particular tag, or combination of tags, set. Tags may be of a permanent nature, e.g. partitioning your database into different categories, or temporary, e.g. to create lists of people you need to 'phone. Tag settings are saved (in the index file) between invocations of the program.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Use the "Define Tag" {\b (Psion-Shift-N) }to create a new tag. In the dialog specify the full name (up to 30 characters) and a "short code" (2 alphabetic characters) for the tag.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can then apply this tag to the current entry (on the {\i Find }or {\i Show }screens) by typing the 2 character short code with the {\b Control }key down (similar to applying styles in Word). This actually has the effect of toggling the current tag setting, i.e. setting it if it is not already set, clearing it if it is set.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can also set or clear tags using the "Set Tag" {\b (Psion-T) }command. In the dialog you can specify a particular tag, or "All" tags, and the operation to be performed (Setting, Clearing, or Toggling). You can perform the operation on just the current entry, all entries in the database, or all entries in the current found set (if a search has previously been performed).\par To find entries with particular tag settings use the "Find Tags" {\b (Psion-Shift-F) }command. You can specify up to 3 different tags, and search for entries which have {\b all }those tags set, for entries which have {\b any }of those tags set, for entries which have {\b none }of those tags set, or for entries which have {\b not all }those tags set. The found entries are listed in the same way as entries found in a text search.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can modify existing tag definitions, to change their full names and/or short codes, using the "Modify Tag" {\b (Psion-Shift-Y) c}ommand. Entries which have the modified tag set will {\ul not} have their tag settings altered, but will be displayed with the new tag name or short code.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can delete tag definitions no longer required using the "Delete Tag" {\b (Psion-Shift-T) }command. Any entries having the deleted tag set will have this cleared.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 10\tab Deleting Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 A single entry can be deleted using the "Delete" {\b (Psion-D)} command, or by typing the {\b Delete} key on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screens. You are asked to confirm the deletion before the entry is removed from the database.\par You can delete all the entries in a found set, or all the entries in the database, by using the "Delete Range" {\b (Psion-Shift-G)} command. You are asked to confirm the command before any entries are deleted.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 11\tab Linking Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This facility allows more than one entry to share the same common information. The sort of situation where this might be useful is where you have a couple who have the same address and home 'phone numbers, but different work 'phone numbers. Each individual can have their own separate entry, but the common address and home 'phone is only defined in one entry; the other entry refers to this 'parent' entry. If the couple should move then you only need to change the address in one place.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Another way of using linking is to set up a 'parent' entry for a Company, and have several linked entries for individuals in the Company.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can create a new entry linked to an existing 'parent' entry using the "Add Linked Entry" {\b (Psion-L)} command. The 'parent' entry must be the current entry on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screen. The {\i Add} screen will then be displayed, with the field contents derived from the 'parent' shown in grey. These values cannot be modified, but can be replaced by typing new contents.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can link an existing entry to an existing 'parent' using the "Link" {\b (Psion-Shift-L)} command. First select the entry to be linked on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screen and type {\b Psion-Shift-L}. Then select the required parent and type {\b Psion-Shift-L} again.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 An entry can only be linked to a single 'parent', but a 'parent' entry can have any number of other entries linked to it.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 When a linked entry is displayed on the {\i Show} screen then for any of its fields which are blank the contents of the field in the 'parent' entry are shown instead. You can suppress the showing of field contents derived from the 'parent' by entering a single space in the field.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 When you update a linked entry, fields derived from the 'parent' are shown in grey and cannot be changed, only replaced.\par You can remove a link from an entry with the "Remove Link" {\b (Psion-Shift-U)} command. You are asked to confirm the removal.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 12\tab Counting Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The "Show Number" {\b (Psion-N)} command on the Entries menu displays briefly the total number of records in the database in the bottom right corner of the screen.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 13\tab DTMF Dialling\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can dial the current entry on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screen by typing {\b Dial (Psion-Help)}. This displays the {\i Dialling} screen which lists all the telephone numbers for that entry. Select the number you wish to dial using the {\b Up} and {\b Down} keys.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\b Tab} key dials the number exactly as shown. {\b Enter} prefixes the "dial out" code, e.g. to make an outside call via a switchboard. Type {\b c} to automatically prefix the account number and PIN for a BT charge card call (or similar). Type {\b d} to dial the number with the code and terminator for a special service (such as BT Star Services Call Diversion). Type {\b Escape} to quit from the {\i Dial} screen.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 After any of these buttons are pressed there is a short delay; this allows you to position the loudspeaker correctly for successful dialling. You can dial further digits by pressing any of the {\b 0} to {\b 9} and {\b *}, {\b #} keys while the {\i Dial} screen is displayed.\par 'Phone numbers are dialled with correct International prefixes and country codes providing they have been entered in standard Psion format, as described in the Psion User Manual.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Note the {\b c} and{\b  d} buttons will not be displayed or activated until you have set up an account number and PIN or service code; use the "Dialling Setup" {\b (Psion-Shift-D)} command to display the Dialling Setup Dialog. When setting up the Divert Code specify both the code prefix and the terminator, separated by a space. E.g. for Star Services "Divert on No Reply" you should set {\b *61* #}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can also set the code required to obtain an outside line. E.g. if you have to dial {\b 9} and then wait for a second dial tone you should set {\b 9,}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The other fields allow you to specify the duration of the tones, the pause between tones, and the length of a delay; units are {\up6 1}/{\dn6 32}s of a second. You can also set the volume of the tones; allowed values are 0 to 5, with 5 the {\ul quietest}. (5 seems to give the most reliable results with most 'phones.)\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 14\tab Printing\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This program does {\ul not} support direct output to a printer attached to the Psion. However you can output to a file, either in plain text or rich text (RTF) formats. These can then be printed using the built-in {\b Word} application, or transferred to an external PC for printing. RTF files contain formatting instructions in addition to the text, which can be interpreted by compatible Word Processors (e.g. Microsoft Word).\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The RTF output generated by this program for various layouts has been tested using Microsoft Word for Macintosh V4.0. This interprets all the formatting commands to produce 'correct' printout, {\ul except} that page orientation commands (\\landscape and \\portrait) are ignored, and orientation has to be set manually within Word before printing. Some other Word Processors may not interpret the formatting commands fully and correctly; in particular I have seen reports that Word Perfect is not very good at interpreting RTF. Hence it is recommended that Word (if available) should be used to interpret and print RTF output\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 14.1\tab Defining Print Layouts\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Before you can print you must define at least one layout, to specify which fields you wish to be printed, and how you want the page laid out. Use the "New Layout" {\b (Psion-Shift-W)} command to display the "Print Layout" dialog.\par Each layout should be given a unique name, which you can type directly into the {\b Name} field. To change other values you must select the appropriate line of the dialog, using the {\b Up} and {\b Down} keys, and type {\b Tab} to enter the sub-dialog.\par The {\b Page} sub-dialog allows you to specify the width and height of the paper (all dimensions are in inches) and the orientation (portrait or landscape). You can also specify the number of rows and columns on each page.\par If {\b Rows} is 0 then page breaks are inserted automatically when there is not enough room for the next entry. If {\b Columns} is greater than 1 then entries are printed in 'snaking' columns. This option would be suitable for making a 'hard copy' listing of your entries.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If {\b Rows} is 1 then entries are printed one per page. This would be suitable for printing addresses onto envelopes.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If {\b Rows} is greater than 1 then entries are printed inside {\b Rows} * {\b Columns} equally sized and spaced 'cells' per page. This would be suitable for printing addresses onto sheets of labels.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can also specify if the field labels and/or empty fields are printed.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 In the {\b Margins }sub-dialog you can set the margins round the whole page and the position of each entry. {\b Horizontal offset} is the offset of the text from the left margin, column boundary, or cell boundary; field labels are always printed flush against the left margin. If {\b Rows} is 0 then {\b Vertical offset} sets the vertical spacing between entries, otherwise it sets the offset of each entry from the top margin or the top of the cell.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\b Font} sub-dialog allows you to specify the font name, size, and attributes. As well as the {\b Name} of the font (e.g. "Times" or "Helvetica") you need to specify the {\b Family} to which it belongs (e.g. "roman" or "swiss"). {\b Name} and {\b Family} can be any combination which the Word Processor on your PC can recognise; you can find out about available fonts by generating an RTF file from your PC's Word Processor and examining the entries in the {\b \\fonttbl} section. The sub-dialog also enables you to set the font size and whether the field labels and/or main text are printed in bold and/or italic.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The {\b Fields} sub-dialog allows you to set which fields are printed. You can specify all fields, or up to 7 selected fields (in any order). If you select "Name OR Organisation" then either the designated "Name" field will be printed, or if this is empty the designated "Organisation" field.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Existing layouts can be changed using the "Modify Layout" command {\b (Psion-Shift-M)} or deleted using the "Delete Layout" command {\b (Psion-Shift-E)}. You can create a new layout based on an existing one using the "Copy Layout" command {\b (Psion-Shift-C)}.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 14.2\tab Printing Entries\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If you wish to output the print file directly to your PC then you must first connect and activate the serial link.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The "Print" {\b (Psion-P)} command brings up the Print dialog. This allows you to specify:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab whether {\b All} entries in the database are printed, or selected {\b Individual} entries, or the {\b Found} subset (if you had previously performed a search);\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab which layout to use;\par \bullet \tab whether to generate a plain {\b Text} file or an {\b Rtf} file;\par \bullet \tab the drive and specification for the {\b Destination }file;\par \bullet \tab whether you are printing via a {\b Host} Apple {\b Mac} or IBM {\b PC}.\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If you specified {\b All} or {\b Found }entries, then these are printed immediately you terminate the dialog with {\b Return}. \par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If you specified {\b Individual} entries then you must select each entry that you wish to print (on the {\i Find} screen) and use the "Print Entry"{\b  (Psion-P)} command. When all required entries have been printed you terminate printing with the "Finish Printing" {\b (Psion-Shift-P)} command.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 14.3\tab Setting Printer Defaults\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can set default values for:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab the destination drive and directory for print files;\par \bullet \tab the file format (Text or RTF);\par \bullet \tab the host type (Mac or PC);\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 using the "Print Setup" {\b (Psion-Y)} command. Also in this dialog you can specify how to print the 'phone symbol which appears in some labels. You need to specify the name and family of an appropriate special symbol font which includes a 'phone symbol in its character set, and the equivalent character in a normal character set. E.g. on the Mac you can use font Zapf Dingbats (family nil), which prints a "%" character as a 'phone symbol.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Note that if you wish to specify a default directory on the PC then you must have the serial link activated when performing this dialog. If you subsequently attempt to print with the link disconnected then the Print dialog will not offer this remote drive and directory. However if you then print to a local drive on the Psion this will {\ul not} supersede the default setting.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 15\tab Editing Labels\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can modify the labels template by using the "Edit Labels" {\b (Psion-E)} command. The {\i Edit Labels} screen is then displayed. Keys operate as on the {\i Add} and {\i Update} screens. Use {\b Shift-Dial (Psion-Shift-Help)} to insert the 'phone symbol at the start of a label, to indicate that the field is diallable.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 It is not advisable to add or remove labels in the middle of the template, as data in existing records will become misaligned with the labels. It is okay however to add new fields to the end of the template.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 16\tab Exporting Records\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can export all or a selection of records in a database to a text or data file, using the "Export" {\b (Psion-R)} command. This displays a dialog enabling you to specify:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab The file to which the records are to be exported;\par \bullet \tab Whether the output file is a {\b Data} or {\b Text} file;\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab (For text files) the field delimitter: {\b Comma}, {\b Tab, }or a {\b Custom} character with a specified code;\par \bullet \tab Whether {\b All} records in the database are to be exported, or {\b Individually} selected records, or (if appropriate) the current set of {\b Found} records;\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab Whether field labels are to be exported (for data files the labels are written into the Descriptive Record so that {\b Data} and similar programs will display them normally, for text files the labels are written as the initial record);\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab Whether {\b All} record fields are to be exported, or just {\b Selected} fields.\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If "Export Selected Fields" is specified then up to four further dialogs are dislayed, allowing you to specify up to 32 fields to be exported. Type {\b Return} to add the selected fields to the list of fields to be exported, or {\b Escape} if no further fields are to be added to the list. The fields are exported in the order they are entered on the dialogs, which may be different from the order in the record template. The same field may appear more than once in the list.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can cancel an export command by typing {\b Escape} on the initial dialog or the {\ul first} field selection dialog.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If export {\b All }or {\b Found} records was specified, then these records are exported, and a progress count is displayed. Otherwise you can export individual records by selecting them on the {\i Find} or {\i Show} screen and using the "Export Entry" {\b (Psion-R)} command. When all required records have been exported close the output file by using the "Finish Exporting" {\b (Psion-Shift-R)} command.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 17\tab Importing Records\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can merge records from data or delimited text files into an Address Book database, using the "Import" {\b (Psion-Shift-I)} command. This displays a dialog enabling you to specify:\par \pard\plain \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \f16 \bullet \tab The file from which the records are to be imported;\par \bullet \tab Whether the input file is a {\b Data} or {\b Text} file;\par \pard \s5\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx720 \bullet \tab (For text files) the field delimitter: {\b Comma}, {\b Tab, }or a {\b Custom} character with a specified code;\par \bullet \tab Whether {\b All} fields in the imported records are to be merged into the database, or just {\b Selected }fields.\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If import {\b Selected} fields is specified then further dialogs are displayed to enable you to specify which fields are to be imported. The fixed labels on the left side of the dialogs correspond to the field labels in the input file (for text files or data files which do have labels defined these labels are shown as "Field 1", "Field 2", etc.). On the right hand side select the Address Book field into which you wish data from the corresponding input field to be written, or leave blank if you do not want data to be imported from a particular field. These fields are initialised to match the names of fields in the input file.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 You can type {\b Escape} on any of the dialogs to abandon the import operation. Otherwise, after you type {\b Enter} on the final dialog, the records are imported into the database while a progress count is displayed. \par When the importing is completed the newly imported records are listed on the {\i Find} screen as a {\b Found} set. This then enables you to perform operations on them as a group, e.g. to mark them with a particular tag.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 18\tab Upgrading\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 18.1\tab Converting from Address Book V3.0\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 No special conversion is required when upgrading from V3.0, since the file formats are identical.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 18.2\tab Converting from Address Book V2.x\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If you have a database that was created using {\b Address Book }V2.x, then you must first convert the file before you can manipulate it with {\b Address Book} V3.1, since the  formats of the associated index files are different (the formats of the main database files are identical: standard Psion data format).\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Copy the file {\b CONVADB2.OPO }to {\b \\OPO }on the {\ul same} drive as you installed the main application. Open the database with {\b Address Book } in the normal way. A dialog box should appear asking if you wish to convert a V2.x file. If you respond with {\b Enter} then the program will convert the index file (and create a .MAP file), and then open the file displaying entries on the {\i Find} screen as normal.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Once all your databases have been converted you can remove CONVADB2.OPO from your disk.\par \pard\plain \s254\sa120\sl240\keepn \b\f21 18.3\tab Converting from Address Book V1.0\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 It is not possible to convert directly from V1.0 to V3.1. However you can use any V2.x to convert the file initially to a form which V3.1 can convert, as above. In case of difficulty please contact the author.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 19\tab Problems and Troubleshooting\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The most likely problems to be encountered are associated with running out of memory. This is most likely when attempting to display a new screen or dialog. The program is designed to trap such occurrences and either abandon what it was trying to do or exit gracefully.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If the program should exit in an uncontrolled way, or has to killed off (because it appears to be locked up), then when it restarts it may display the message "Index file is obsolete". This is the result of a check it performs to see whether the database has been modified by another application, which would make it inconsistent with the index. If the database has not in fact been modified then it should be okay to continue.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If the program should start behaving erratically (e.g. listing entries in wrong order or missing out some entries) this may indicate corruption or inconsistency in the index file. In such cases it is best to exit, and delete the index file. Then when the database is reopened {\b Address Book} will rebuild the index.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 I will be pleased to receive any queries or comments about this program. I can be contacted via:\par \pard\plain \s7\fi-2160\li2160\sa240\sl240\keep\tx2160 \f16 CIX:\tab jdawson\par Compuserve:\tab 100415,370\par E-mail:\tab John.W.Dawson@baesema.co.uk\par 'Phone:\tab +44 118 9666185\par \pard \s7\fi-2160\li2160\sa240\sl240\keep\tx2160 Snail mail:\tab 19, St Edward's Rd.\line Reading\line Berkshire\line RG6 1NL\line England\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Note that there have been problems in the past with my office mail system, so if you do not receive any response from messages sent to the above e-mail address, please try my CIX address as an alternative. Internet address is:\par \pard\plain \s7\fi-2160\li2160\sa240\sl240\keep\tx2160 \f16 \tab jdawson@cix.compulink.co.uk\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Please 'phone outside working hours if possible, and not after 11pm UK time!\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 20\tab File Formats and Compatibility\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 By default {\b Address Book} files are created in \\ADD directories, and have extension ".ADD", as in V1.0. Unlike V1.0, V3.1 uses a standard DBF format which should be compatible with the built-in {\b Database} and other applications. Hence these other applications should be able to {\ul read} files created by {\b Address Book}.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 However it is not recommended that other applications are used to {\ul modify} or {\ul add} to files being maintained by {\b Address Book}. The reason for this is that {\b Address Book} maintains information in a separate 'index' file, which it uses to list entries alphabetically. This 'index' file must be updated in step with changes to the main database file.\par If the database file is made inconsistent with the 'index' file, then it is possible to reconstruct the index, though this does take significant time. Also any links and tags which have been set up will be lost, since information about these is also held in the index file. No actual data is lost, and the links can be restored manually, i.e. using the {\b Psion-Shift-L} command.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 The 'index' file has the same name as the main DBF file, but with extension ".IDX". It is normally created on the same drive as the DBF file, except where this is a flash SSD. In this case the index file is created on the internal drive. There is also a ".MAP" file created on the same drive as the 'index' file.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 21\tab Registration\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 I am making this program available as Shareware. The software as distributed is fully operational. However if you find this program useful then I would ask you to send a donation (suggested \'a310 Sterling) to a charity I am closely involved with. {\b Reading Steiner School Project} (Registered Charity No. 297857) is developing education in the Reading area based on the work of the Austrian educationalist and philosopher Rudolf Steiner.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Please make cheques etc. payable to R.S.S.P., and send them to:\par \pard\plain \s7\fi-2160\li2160\sa240\sl240\keep\tx2160 \f16 \tab Department ADBK\line Alder Bridge School\line Mill Lane\line Padworth\line Reading\line England\line RG7 4JU\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Non-UK users might prefer to make a donation to a Steiner school in their home country; I can provide addresses on request.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 If you provide an e-mail (preferred) or postal address with your registration then I will send you instructions on how to remove the registration request from the start-up screen.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Please do not send any technical queries etc. to the School!\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Please do distribute this program freely, providing you do not make any charge (other than to cover reasonable costs of distribution) and you include these notes.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 22\tab Future Developments\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 I have developed this program for my own use, so its facilities and operation very much reflect my own requirements.  And many thanks for the useful suggestions I have received from those who tried out earlier versions. \par I have now implemented all the major functionality I originally envisaged. However I am still very open to suggestions for improvements and enhancements which would make the program useful to a wider range of users.\par \pard\plain \s255\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 23\tab Thanks\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 To those who have made invaluable technical information available via CIX and CIS.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Particular thanks to Tom Dolbilin for publishing details of implementing Link Paste in OPL, and to Andy Clarkson for his excellent OPP (OPL pre-processor) which has enabled me to build the program as a single module, as well as simplifying manipulation of complex data structures and calling OS services.\par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Thanks to Steve Litchfield for his tireless efforts distributing and promoting Psion Shareware.\par To all those who paid registrations for earlier versions, and provided encouragement and ideas for future development. \par \pard \s3\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 {\b \par }\pard\plain \s26\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 \par }